          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Goneril's loving emotional state toward Lear as true. The rest of her turn amplifies the claimed degree of that love rather than changing the expression into another kind of act.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The occurrence does not undertake, renew, or invoke an obligation. Goneril describes the supposed magnitude of her filial love, but she does not position herself as bound to any course of conduct. Lear's announced distribution of bounty makes this a consequential answer, yet that material belongs to his offer and does not convert her avowal into a promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.—Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal words occur within an extended, rhetorically ordered response to Lear's formal demand. Nothing in the supplied scene represents them as a spontaneous or reflex-like affective discharge; the superlatives and intensity alone do not establish that mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.84,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the expression functions as a competitive and persuasive answer in Lear's public love test. It helps advance Goneril's claim to the bounty Lear has explicitly tied to professing the greatest love, although this strategic function remains secondary to the direct avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Of all these bounds, even from this line to this,\\nWith shadowy forests and with champains rich’d,\\nWith plenteous rivers and wide-skirted meads,\\nWe make thee lady:\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public royal assembly, Lear asks each daughter to say how much she loves him and explicitly links the answers to the allocation of her dower. Goneril, called on first, answers with an elaborate superlative profession of filial love.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No commitment with identifiable terms is created or affirmed by the focal expression. Lear accepts Goneril's profession as a successful answer and immediately grants her territory, but this is uptake of her avowal within his love test, not acceptance of a promise. Later supplied passages characterize the elder sisters as having wronged and harmed Lear, which casts doubt on sincerity but does not retroactively remove the original utterance's avowal force.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as William Shakespeare's King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly establishes the public, competitive setting and later conduct inconsistent with the profession, but it cannot conclusively determine Goneril's private emotional state at the exact moment of speaking. Later wrongdoing is evidence against sincerity, not proof that no affection whatsoever existed then.\"}"
        }
